// Practice quiz: Responsive, mobile-first layout
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/html-and-css/responsive-mobile-first-layout

::o1 what min-width means::When does @media (min-width\: 768px) apply its rules? {
=When the viewport is 768px wide or wider #Correct. A min-width query matches at its width and everything above it.
~Only when the viewport is exactly 768px #Not quite. The query matches a range, from 768px upward.
~When the viewport is narrower than 768px #Not quite. That is what a max-width query does.
~On phones only #Not quite. Media queries test the viewport width, not the kind of device.
}

::o1 mobile-first base::In mobile-first CSS, where do the one-column phone styles go? {
=In the base styles, outside any media query #Correct. Mobile first writes the narrow layout as the base and adds min-width queries for wider screens.
~Inside a max-width query at the end #Not quite. That is the desktop-first approach.
~Inside a min-width query for 320px #Not quite. The base needs no query, so it applies everywhere unless a wider query overrides it.
~In a separate mobile stylesheet only phones load #Not quite. One stylesheet with min-width queries serves every width.
}

::o1 query order::A stylesheet has @media (min-width\: 1200px) with four columns, followed by @media (min-width\: 800px) with two columns. What does a 1400px screen show? {
=Two columns, because both queries match and the later rule wins #Correct. Write min-width queries from small to large so the widest match comes last.
~Four columns, because 1200px is closer to 1400px #Not quite. The cascade does not pick the closest breakpoint; the later matching rule wins.
~One column, because the queries cancel out #Not quite. Matching queries never cancel; the later rule overrides.
~Six columns, because the counts add up #Not quite. Column counts are replaced, not added.
}

::o2 missing viewport meta::A page has mobile-first CSS but no viewport meta element. What does a phone usually show? {
=A layout made for about 980px, shrunk down so the text is tiny #Correct. Without the element, mobile browsers lay out at a desktop-like width and scale it to fit the screen.
~The one-column base layout at normal size #Not quite. That needs the viewport meta element with width\=device-width.
~A blank page #Not quite. The page renders; it is just laid out too wide and shrunk.
~The page with no CSS at all #Not quite. CSS still applies; the layout width is the problem.
}

::o3 fixing sideways scroll::At 400px a page scrolls sideways because a banner image has width 700px. What is the best fix? {
=Give the image max-width 100% and height auto #Correct. The image then shrinks to fit its container and keeps its proportions.
~Add overflow-x hidden to the body #Not quite. That hides the scrollbar and cuts off part of the image.
~Make the font size smaller #Not quite. The image, not the text, is too wide.
~Add a min-width media query for 700px #Not quite. A query for wider screens does nothing at 400px.
}
